{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Path aliases are a useful feature of TypeScript that allows you to define a shortcut for a path that could be distant in your project's directory structure. This can help you avoid long relative imports in your code and make it easier to refactor your project's structure. This is especially useful for avoiding long relative imports in your code."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, TanStack Start does not include path aliases. However, you can easily add them to your project by updating your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsconfig.json"},{"type":"text","value":" file in the root of your project and adding the following configuration:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"baseUrl\": \".\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"~/*\": [\"./src/*\"]\n    }\n  }\n}","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example, we've defined the path alias "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"~/*"},{"type":"text","value":" that maps to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"./src/*"},{"type":"text","value":" directory. This means that you can now import files from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src"},{"type":"text","value":" directory using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"~"},{"type":"text","value":" prefix."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After updating your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsconfig.json"},{"type":"text","value":" file, configure your build tool so it resolves the same path aliases."}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"bundler"},"children":[{"type":"component","name":"tab-panel","tagName":"md-tab-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-tab-slug":"vite","data-tab-index":"0","data-content":"mixed"},"children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"vite-8","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vite 8"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vite 8+ has "},{"type":"link","href":"https://vite.dev/config/shared-options#resolve-tsconfigpaths","children":[{"type":"text","value":"built-in support for path aliases"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which is disabled by default. To enable it, simply add the following configuration to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vite.config.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  resolve: {\n    // This enables built-in support for path aliases defined in tsconfig.json\n    tsconfigPaths: true,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"vite-7-and-earlier","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vite 7 and earlier"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For Vite 7 and earlier, install the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vite-tsconfig-paths"},{"type":"text","value":" plugin to enable path aliases in your TanStack Start project. You can do this by running the following command:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install -D vite-tsconfig-paths","lang":"sh"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now, you'll need to update your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vite.config.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file to include the following:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport viteTsConfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    // this is the plugin that enables path aliases\n    viteTsConfigPaths({\n      projects: ['./tsconfig.json'],\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tab-panel","tagName":"md-tab-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-tab-slug":"rsbuild","data-tab-index":"1","data-content":"mixed"},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rsbuild reads the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paths"},{"type":"text","value":" field from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsconfig.json"},{"type":"text","value":" by default. No extra config is needed when your aliases live in the root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsconfig.json"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you use a custom tsconfig file, point Rsbuild at it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source.tsconfigPath"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// rsbuild.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  source: {\n    tsconfigPath: './tsconfig.custom.json',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"}]}],"properties":{"data-attributes":"{\"tabs\":[{\"slug\":\"vite\",\"name\":\"vite\"},{\"slug\":\"rsbuild\",\"name\":\"rsbuild\"}]}","data-bundler-meta":"{\"bundlers\":[\"vite\",\"rsbuild\"]}"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once this configuration has completed, you'll now be able to import files using the path alias like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/routes/posts/$postId/edit.tsx\nimport { Input } from '~/components/ui/input'\n\n// instead of\n\nimport { Input } from '../../../components/ui/input'","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nPath aliases are a useful feature of TypeScript that allows you to define a shortcut for a path that could be distant in your project's directory structure. This can help you avoid long relative imports in your code and make it easier to refactor your project's structure. This is especially useful for avoiding long relative imports in your code.\n\nBy default, TanStack Start does not include path aliases. However, you can easily add them to your project by updating your `tsconfig.json` file in the root of your project and adding the following configuration:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"baseUrl\": \".\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"~/*\": [\"./src/*\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we've defined the path alias `~/*` that maps to the `./src/*` directory. This means that you can now import files from the `src` directory using the `~` prefix.\n\nAfter updating your `tsconfig.json` file, configure your build tool so it resolves the same path aliases.\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"bundler\" -->\n\n# Vite\n\n## Vite 8\n\nVite 8+ has [built-in support for path aliases](https://vite.dev/config/shared-options#resolve-tsconfigpaths), which is disabled by default. To enable it, simply add the following configuration to your `vite.config.ts` file:\n\n```ts\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  resolve: {\n    // This enables built-in support for path aliases defined in tsconfig.json\n    tsconfigPaths: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Vite 7 and earlier\n\nFor Vite 7 and earlier, install the `vite-tsconfig-paths` plugin to enable path aliases in your TanStack Start project. You can do this by running the following command:\n\n```sh\nnpm install -D vite-tsconfig-paths\n```\n\nNow, you'll need to update your `vite.config.ts` file to include the following:\n\n```ts\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport viteTsConfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    // this is the plugin that enables path aliases\n    viteTsConfigPaths({\n      projects: ['./tsconfig.json'],\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Rsbuild\n\nRsbuild reads the `paths` field from `tsconfig.json` by default. No extra config is needed when your aliases live in the root `tsconfig.json`.\n\nIf you use a custom tsconfig file, point Rsbuild at it with `source.tsconfigPath`:\n\n```ts\n// rsbuild.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  source: {\n    tsconfigPath: './tsconfig.custom.json',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nOnce this configuration has completed, you'll now be able to import files using the path alias like so:\n\n```ts\n// app/routes/posts/$postId/edit.tsx\nimport { Input } from '~/components/ui/input'\n\n// instead of\n\nimport { Input } from '../../../components/ui/input'\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: path-aliases\ntitle: Path Aliases\n---\n\nPath aliases are a useful feature of TypeScript that allows you to define a shortcut for a path that could be distant in your project's directory structure. This can help you avoid long relative imports in your code and make it easier to refactor your project's structure. This is especially useful for avoiding long relative imports in your code.\n\nBy default, TanStack Start does not include path aliases. However, you can easily add them to your project by updating your `tsconfig.json` file in the root of your project and adding the following configuration:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"baseUrl\": \".\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"~/*\": [\"./src/*\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we've defined the path alias `~/*` that maps to the `./src/*` directory. This means that you can now import files from the `src` directory using the `~` prefix.\n\nAfter updating your `tsconfig.json` file, configure your build tool so it resolves the same path aliases.\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"bundler\" -->\n\n# Vite\n\n## Vite 8\n\nVite 8+ has [built-in support for path aliases](https://vite.dev/config/shared-options#resolve-tsconfigpaths), which is disabled by default. To enable it, simply add the following configuration to your `vite.config.ts` file:\n\n```ts\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  resolve: {\n    // This enables built-in support for path aliases defined in tsconfig.json\n    tsconfigPaths: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Vite 7 and earlier\n\nFor Vite 7 and earlier, install the `vite-tsconfig-paths` plugin to enable path aliases in your TanStack Start project. You can do this by running the following command:\n\n```sh\nnpm install -D vite-tsconfig-paths\n```\n\nNow, you'll need to update your `vite.config.ts` file to include the following:\n\n```ts\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport viteTsConfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    // this is the plugin that enables path aliases\n    viteTsConfigPaths({\n      projects: ['./tsconfig.json'],\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Rsbuild\n\nRsbuild reads the `paths` field from `tsconfig.json` by default. No extra config is needed when your aliases live in the root `tsconfig.json`.\n\nIf you use a custom tsconfig file, point Rsbuild at it with `source.tsconfigPath`:\n\n```ts\n// rsbuild.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  source: {\n    tsconfigPath: './tsconfig.custom.json',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nOnce this configuration has completed, you'll now be able to import files using the path alias like so:\n\n```ts\n// app/routes/posts/$postId/edit.tsx\nimport { Input } from '~/components/ui/input'\n\n// instead of\n\nimport { Input } from '../../../components/ui/input'\n```\n"}
